finding: 1. src/cli/publish-fly.ts:283–284,403–415 — The shared inventory gate accepts a stopped, cordoned Machine as the sole persistent server. Cordoning removes the Machine from Fly Proxy routing (https://fly.io/docs/machines/api/machines-resource/), so canonical service autostart does not establish a usable proxy cold start, while its attached volume continues billing. In a scratch copy, changing only the fixture state to stopped and adding cordoned:true made both the absent-app and authorized-existing-app success tests resolve. This is acceptance of an already observed state, not the accepted post-read race or a claim that ordinary flyctl deploy produces it. 2. src/cli/publish-fly.ts:271–276,301,410–415 — The post-deploy gate does not tie the observed Machine's image to the deployed artifact. The canonical init object is empty, leaving the image to choose the process that reads /data and handles SIGTERM. In a scratch copy, replacing config.image with registry.fly.io/other-app:other-server and setting image_ref:null still let both success tests resolve. A variable artifact identity is legitimate before redeploy; ignoring it after deploy also accepts a different server process or missing resolved-image evidence on the sole Machine. The synthetic replay establishes acceptance, not that pinned flyctl normally returns the wrong image.
